WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:15.180
And now, I'd like to introduce today's speaker, Professor Sam Savage.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:20.240
Dr. Sam Savage is an executive director of probabilitymanagement.org,

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:26.240
a 501C3 non-profit devoted to the communication and calculation of uncertainty.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.565
The organization has received funding from Chevron,

00:00:29.565 --> 00:00:32.370
Lockheed Martin, PG&amp;E and others.

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:35.210
And he is joined on the board by Harry Markowitz,

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.645
Nobel Laureate in Economics.

00:00:37.645 --> 00:00:39.485
Dr. Savage is the author of

00:00:39.485 --> 00:00:43.720
The Flaw of Averages: Why We Underestimate Risk in the Face of Uncertainty.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:49.225
He's an adjunct professor in civil environmental engineering at Stanford University.

00:00:49.225 --> 00:00:54.195
He's the inventor of the Stochastic Information Packets also known as SIP,

00:00:54.195 --> 00:00:57.545
an auditable data array for conveying uncertainty.

00:00:57.545 --> 00:01:03.190
Dr. Savage received his PhD in computational complexity from Yale University.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.330
My name is Pinky Medina,

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:06.500
and I'm the program manager,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.470
of the academic programs team at the Stanford Center for Professional Development,

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.065
and I will be moderating today's webinar and the Q&amp;A session.

00:01:14.065 --> 00:01:17.335
Now, I'd like to turn the floor over to Sam.

00:01:17.335 --> 00:01:20.250
Thanks, Pinky. So,

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.405
here's my first question for the audience.

00:01:22.405 --> 00:01:29.070
have you had at least one statistics course in your career, yes or no?

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:36.315
Typically if you have,

00:01:36.315 --> 00:01:40.002
it doesn't take more than 45 minutes to repair the damage.

00:01:40.002 --> 00:01:45.000
Okay, we have a lot of people who have taken statistics.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.910
Let's go on to the next question.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:52.565
If you have,

00:01:52.565 --> 00:01:55.580
one, was it the high point of your week?

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:14.480
you still suffer from PTSD, or other.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.020
Other. Okay. Well, I don't know what other means,

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.380
but I'll proceed as if you

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.270
have not

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.035
necessarily gotten anything out of your statistics course,

00:02:24.035 --> 00:02:27.630
and will sort of start from scratch.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:32.185
So given that we probably have a very wide range of backgrounds,

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:35.700
I wanna sort of level the playing field by starting

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.970
with something that everybody understands.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:46.630
These are the differential equations of motion of a bicycle.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:50.300
And the point here is that you do not

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:53.774
learn to ride a bicycle through the seat of your intellect,

00:02:53.774 --> 00:02:55.765
but through the seat of your pants.

00:02:55.765 --> 00:02:59.370
So, my philosophy in teaching and in this webinar,

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:04.140
is to connect the seat of the intellect to the seat of the pants.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:08.485
And I call that Limbic Analytics.

00:03:08.485 --> 00:03:13.245
here's a sensitive rendition from my book

00:03:13.245 --> 00:03:14.960
from the cartoonist, Jeff Danziger,

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.535
of someone, engaged in limbic analytics.

00:03:18.535 --> 00:03:24.630
now, let's start with,

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.330
let's dive right in

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.510
to a project that has,

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:33.000
we're doing project risk analysis here,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.790
a project that has uncertain durations.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:41.025
So, here I have a project,

00:03:41.025 --> 00:03:43.595
let's say we're building out a website,

00:03:43.595 --> 00:03:49.350
and we have 10 pages that need to be completed.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:52.870
Page one could be product one,

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:53.950
then we have product two,

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.975
then we have the shopping cart page,

00:03:55.975 --> 00:03:58.015
then we have a legal page.

00:03:58.015 --> 00:04:04.250
these 10 pages are being created in parallel by 10 separate teams,

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:07.100
and it's the easiest kind of project to imagine because there's no,

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.190
you don't have to finish this one before you do this one.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:12.650
But out of these 10 teams, and of course,

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.440
you're uncertain how long it will take for each team to finish,

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:24.085
but you can't go live until all 10 teams are done.

00:04:24.085 --> 00:04:28.230
So, what typically happens now is the boss walks in and says,

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.560
"When do we go live?"

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.170
And you typically say, "I don't know boss,

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:33.950
because I don't know how long team one will take,

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:35.915
or team two, or team three."

00:04:35.915 --> 00:04:38.710
And then the boss will say something like,

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:42.350
"Give me a number," and

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:45.870
I don't know if any of your organizations use that phrase, but

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:49.330
"Give me a number" is the fork and road to hell, because

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.370
once you've given a specific number,

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.725
you really can't do any reasonable analysis.

00:04:53.725 --> 00:04:56.345
Now, let's just remember what we have here.

00:04:56.345 --> 00:05:01.495
Each of these times to completion is uncertain,

00:05:01.495 --> 00:05:04.695
but everybody agrees that on average,

00:05:04.695 --> 00:05:07.265
each one will take six weeks.

00:05:07.265 --> 00:05:10.650
Given that,

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:15.560
what's the chance of finishing in six weeks?

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:33.750
So, 100 percent, 50 percent,

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:37.690
taking a while for this poll to fill in.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:43.785
Okay, we have a winner.

00:05:43.785 --> 00:05:48.190
And so, the winner,

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:50.440
oh this is great, I hope we can save these statistics,

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:54.295
the winner is 50 percent. All right.

00:05:54.295 --> 00:05:57.610
well, that's not right by the way but it is the winner,

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:00.050
it had almost double what the others did.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:01.855
Okay. So, let's move on now,

00:06:01.855 --> 00:06:04.290
and let me explain

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:09.715
that the actual answer is one in a thousand.

00:06:09.715 --> 00:06:13.780
And, it's not just enough to know that it's one in a thousand,

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.750
which most of you didn't

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:20.470
but you got to be able to explain it to a 10 year old, or your boss.

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.745
so look at it this way.

00:06:22.745 --> 00:06:28.710
The only way in which this project finishes in six weeks or

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:36.800
less is if all 10 of these finish in six weeks or less.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.830
And if you imagine each one had a 50/50 chance of being greater or less than six weeks,

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:44.880
then that's like flipping 10 heads in a row on a coin,

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.350
which is actually 1,000 in 24,

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:49.930
but hey, I will live with one in 1,000.

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:53.290
Got that? Now actually,

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.215
this is pretty serious stuff.

00:06:56.215 --> 00:06:59.520
The bulk of people in our little poll said, "Oh yeah,

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.110
50/50 chance will be done within six

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:07.850
weeks," when there's a one in 1,000 chance of being done in six weeks.

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:12.785
So that's actually not close enough even for government work.

00:07:12.785 --> 00:07:16.190
This is what I call the flaw of averages.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:18.095
Here's another cartoon from my book.

00:07:18.095 --> 00:07:22.180
This is a statistician drowning in a river that's on average three feet deep.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:29.360
The flaw of averages states that plans based on average assumptions are wrong on average.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:31.690
And let me say this is based on

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:36.510
a well-known mathematical principle called Jensen's Inequality.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:40.105
but even most mathematicians haven't heard of that.

00:07:40.105 --> 00:07:42.530
So, it is a very important topic,

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:45.360
and it requires some public relations,

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.550
for people to understand it.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:50.500
So, let me give you another more sobering example.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:54.330
Consider a drunk wandering back and forth in a busy highway,

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:57.625
his average position is the center line.

00:07:57.625 --> 00:08:01.780
So, the state of the drunkard at his average position is alive,

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:04.695
but on average, he's dead.

00:08:04.695 --> 00:08:07.325
Again not close enough even for government work.

00:08:07.325 --> 00:08:11.490
Now, because this is really a public relations campaign on my part,

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.945
and that's really,

00:08:13.945 --> 00:08:15.985
the non-profit has a lot to do with that as well.

00:08:15.985 --> 00:08:20.265
I've come up with an audio logo for the flaw of averages.

00:08:20.265 --> 00:08:22.955
So, everyone know what an audio logo is?

00:08:22.955 --> 00:08:26.100
this is the audio logo for Intel.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.550
[MUSIC]

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:30.100
I'm sure you all recognize that.

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:33.050
So, here's the audio logo for the flaw of averages.

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:43.205
[MUSIC] Now, let's go,

00:08:43.205 --> 00:08:46.230
sort of discuss

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:54.685
how to attack this problem.

00:08:54.685 --> 00:08:57.930
There is a cure for the Flaw of Averages,

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.160
which is really why the

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.720
the nonprofit is around

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.915
and it involves two recent breakthroughs.

00:09:05.915 --> 00:09:08.990
