00:14:38.515 --> 00:14:43.585
But let's make this- let's stick in the plot a little bit here.

00:14:43.585 --> 00:14:50.315
Suppose there were a contract with performance penalties.

00:14:50.315 --> 00:14:55.445
So, okay, someone told them we're done in seven weeks.

00:14:55.445 --> 00:15:01.310
And you agreed to a late penalty of 100,000 per week or fraction thereof if you're late.

00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:03.980
Uh oh, how is that going to look?

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:07.420
Well, if you plug in all the average assumptions,

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:11.705
you're done in six weeks with no penalty, great!

00:15:11.705 --> 00:15:15.165
But I'm not as interested in that as I am in the following.

00:15:15.165 --> 00:15:19.060
I wanna know the average of all 1,000 of these things.

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:20.680
See scenario three, scenario four,

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:22.210
we can do this 1,000 times,

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:27.455
I want the average of those numbers over a thousand scenarios.

00:15:27.455 --> 00:15:29.460
That's what the Flaw of Averages is about.

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:32.410
It says that the number you get when you plug in the average is not

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:35.960
the same as the average overall scenarios.

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:38.120
So remember, plugging the averages,

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:42.255
you're done in six weeks with no penalty,

00:15:42.255 --> 00:15:48.570
but on average, you're done in 7.8 weeks with almost $ 87,000 in penalty.

