00:32:57.090 --> 00:33:00.340
that I've told you you've been using the wrong distribution for your whole life?

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:03.790
No. Simulate, it's smart.

00:33:03.790 --> 00:33:07.175
Garbage in, insight out.

00:33:07.175 --> 00:33:09.440
Let's go to the next step.

00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:13.200
Smarter, optimize.

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:16.710
Let's go back to our

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:19.295
problem over here.

00:33:19.295 --> 00:33:23.220
So, if you've done the right analysis,

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:27.910
you would know now this is a million dollar project is worth $600,000 on average.

00:33:27.910 --> 00:33:30.545
Ain't great.

00:33:30.545 --> 00:33:34.250
Can you do something to make the situation better?

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:42.055
So, let me remind you that any demand over 100 percent,

00:33:42.055 --> 00:33:43.620
over 100,000, excuse me,

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:45.110
which is the average, right?

00:33:45.110 --> 00:33:48.550
Any demand over 100,000 is not gonna be met,

00:33:48.550 --> 00:33:50.860
because you only stock the average down here.

00:33:50.860 --> 00:33:55.480
So, seems to me we should

00:33:55.480 --> 00:34:01.785
increase capacity to above 100,000.

00:34:01.785 --> 00:34:03.855
Well, that might be risky,

00:34:03.855 --> 00:34:05.990
because you have to spend more money to do that, right?

00:34:05.990 --> 00:34:08.240
Yet it's 30 bucks

00:34:08.240 --> 00:34:11.875
a pop for that initial- maybe we should make the capacity smaller.

00:34:11.875 --> 00:34:15.940
Maybe we should leave it as it is. Oh my gosh.

00:34:15.940 --> 00:34:18.910
I don't know what to do. What do you think we should do?

00:34:18.910 --> 00:34:22.160
Let's take a poll.

00:34:23.850 --> 00:34:29.235
So, our average profit is now $600,000.

00:34:29.235 --> 00:34:31.530
To increase that average profit,

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:37.000
should we increase our capacity and spend more money to make bigger capacity?

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:40.135
should we decrease our capacity where we won't spend as much money,

00:34:40.135 --> 00:34:43.075
or should we maintain current capacity?

00:34:43.075 --> 00:34:45.160
What do people think about this?

00:34:45.160 --> 00:35:01.100
Looks like a lot of head scratching going on out there.

00:35:01.100 --> 00:35:03.365
Okay.

00:35:03.365 --> 00:35:07.340
The increased capacity

00:35:07.340 --> 00:35:10.950
wins, got 47 percent,

00:35:10.950 --> 00:35:16.100
decreased capacity about 30 percent and stayed the same as 23 percent.

00:35:16.100 --> 00:35:21.895
Okay, very good. So, let's go back.

00:35:21.895 --> 00:35:24.895
the winners wanted to increase capacity, so let's do that.

00:35:24.895 --> 00:35:29.495
Let's just jack this up to say 120,000.

00:35:29.495 --> 00:35:33.600
Remember what we're trying to beat down here is 600,000.

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:36.560
Here it goes, 120,000,

00:35:36.560 --> 00:35:46.295
our profit dropped- our average profit dropped by more than half, down to 279.

00:35:46.295 --> 00:35:51.500
Now, that's reduced capacity to 80,000,

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:58.600
and it goes up, from 600,000 to 600- almost 80,000.

00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:03.355
Fantastic. Now, let me make a confession.

00:36:03.355 --> 00:36:07.505
This is a problem out of one of my textbooks,

00:36:07.505 --> 00:36:10.475
and I designed this problem,

00:36:10.475 --> 00:36:14.430
so that be more profitable to increase capacity.

00:36:14.430 --> 00:36:17.510
So, I designed the problem and then I simulated it,

00:36:17.510 --> 00:36:21.880
and it said no it's less profitable increase capacity and that I say,

00:36:21.880 --> 00:36:23.045
"Oh, I must be wrong.

00:36:23.045 --> 00:36:25.265
No! I said oh!

00:36:25.265 --> 00:36:27.165
I must have built my model wrong,

00:36:27.165 --> 00:36:29.150
and I went through that phase of denial for,

00:36:29.150 --> 00:36:30.810
you know, 10 or 15 minutes till I found out.

00:36:30.810 --> 00:36:33.295
No, I was wrong.

00:36:33.295 --> 00:36:35.310
The answer to this question is,

00:36:35.310 --> 00:36:37.110
and the next time you see a question like this,

00:36:37.110 --> 00:36:38.285
should we make more?

00:36:38.285 --> 00:36:39.740
Should we make less? Should we?

00:36:39.740 --> 00:36:43.195
The answer is you can't do this in your head,

00:36:43.195 --> 00:36:46.650
so don't try. All right.

00:36:46.650 --> 00:36:52.850
now, let's go to the next phase with this thing and do

00:36:52.850 --> 00:36:59.840
a bunch of simulations for different capacities.

00:36:59.840 --> 00:37:06.185
So, what we've done in this case,

00:37:06.185 --> 00:37:08.990
is we've run seven simulations,

00:37:08.990 --> 00:37:14.600
where we have a 50,000,

00:37:14.600 --> 00:37:18.350
60,000, 70, 80, 90, 100, 110,000 capacity.

00:37:18.350 --> 00:37:24.360
And it's important to understand what we're graphing.

00:37:24.360 --> 00:37:30.730
The blue line is the average profit for those different levels of capacity,

00:37:30.730 --> 00:37:37.670
the red line is the 10 percent- the 10th percentile,

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:47.435
which means there's a 10 percent risk that we will fall below that line, right?

00:37:47.435 --> 00:37:49.655
So, just for example,

00:37:49.655 --> 00:37:52.090
I like to think of this line as risk.

00:37:52.090 --> 00:37:56.465
So.

00:37:56.465 --> 00:37:58.610
Okay. Eighty is the best.

00:37:58.610 --> 00:38:02.660
You can see it in the graph. You make 679,000 on average, yeah.

00:38:02.660 --> 00:38:12.960
But there's a 10 percent chance of making $20,000 or less.

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:16.235
Now, one of the things I have to say here about risk.

00:38:16.235 --> 00:38:18.180
I don't like to use the word risk.

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:20.830
It takes a lot of caveats before you use that word.

00:38:20.830 --> 00:38:22.255
You got to really know what you're talking about.

00:38:22.255 --> 00:38:24.290
Here's a question for you.

00:38:24.290 --> 00:38:28.635
Is there a risk that IBM stock will go down next week?

00:38:28.635 --> 00:38:31.880
Heck no, I've shorted this stuff.

00:38:31.880 --> 00:38:34.385
The risk for me is that it goes up.

00:38:34.385 --> 00:38:36.715
Risk is in the eye of the beholder.

00:38:36.715 --> 00:38:38.895
You must always remember uncertainty,

00:38:38.895 --> 00:38:40.680
while uncertainty is just like,

00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:42.370
you know, stuff happens, right?

00:38:42.370 --> 00:38:44.700
So, is IBM stock price uncertain?

00:38:44.700 --> 00:38:48.940
Yes. Is there a risk that it goes up?

00:38:48.940 --> 00:38:52.465
For me, yes. Is there a risk it goes down?

00:38:52.465 --> 00:38:55.510
For you who own IBM stock, Yes.

00:38:55.510 --> 00:38:58.060
So, I'm a little hesitant

00:38:58.060 --> 00:39:01.410
to really nail down the word risk, but let's look at this.

00:39:01.410 --> 00:39:04.145
We started here at 100,000.

00:39:04.145 --> 00:39:07.400
We were stock, we were- we had a capacity of 100,000.

00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:08.950
Well that only gave us 60 thousand,

00:39:08.950 --> 00:39:11.245
600,000 average profit.

00:39:11.245 --> 00:39:16.860
Oh yeah, and a 10 percent chance of losing almost 600,000.

00:39:16.860 --> 00:39:23.440
So, stocking 100,000 or having a capacity of 100,000 is nuts.

00:39:23.440 --> 00:39:30.105
in fact, why would anyone do anything greater than 80?

00:39:30.105 --> 00:39:35.310
Eighty maximizes your profit and gives you less risk than these other ones.

00:39:35.620 --> 00:39:38.860
So, I got to draw a straight line here.

00:39:38.860 --> 00:39:44.030
Any strategy to the right of that line is nuts.

00:39:44.030 --> 00:39:46.420
Now, let's back up.

00:39:46.420 --> 00:39:48.975
What if I back down to 70,000?

00:39:48.975 --> 00:39:52.660
Well, I lose a little average profit, don't I?

00:39:52.660 --> 00:39:56.160
Came down a little bit, to a little over 600,000.

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:58.990
What about Mike, what about the risk of bad outcomes?

00:39:58.990 --> 00:40:01.980
Well now, look, my worst pot,

00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:06.210
10th percentile outcome and my average are closed up on each other.

00:40:06.210 --> 00:40:08.845
There's almost no uncertainty there.

00:40:08.845 --> 00:40:11.980
So, there's way less risk.

00:40:11.980 --> 00:40:18.590
So, if we have a capacity of 70,000,

00:40:18.590 --> 00:40:21.265
we have minimum risk.

00:40:21.265 --> 00:40:24.450
So, is 70, is 70,000 nuts?

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:27.205
No, this depends on your risk attitude.

00:40:27.205 --> 00:40:30.645
If you want to go for broke and have the highest average profit,

00:40:30.645 --> 00:40:31.965
you go to 80.

00:40:31.965 --> 00:40:36.000
If you wanna cover your butt and minimize the chance of downside, you go to 70.

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:37.570
But, how about less than 70?

00:40:37.570 --> 00:40:42.820
It's nuts because the red line is coming back down and the blue line is coming back down.

00:40:42.820 --> 00:40:46.570
So, when you're doing simulation analysis,

00:40:46.570 --> 00:40:47.810
there may not be one right answer,

00:40:47.810 --> 00:40:49.785
it's going to depend on your risk attitude.

00:40:49.785 --> 00:40:52.855
There's a range of answers that are not nuts.

00:40:52.855 --> 00:40:54.820
Everything else is just crazy here though.

00:40:54.820 --> 00:41:01.390
All right.

00:41:01.390 --> 00:41:06.050
Now, let's move on to operational risk, and

00:41:06.050 --> 00:41:09.505
we've been doing some exciting work,

00:41:09.505 --> 00:41:12.270
with PG&amp;E here in Northern California.

00:41:12.270 --> 00:41:15.950
There's an article in the December 2016, OR,

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:17.995
MS Today, and I believe there,

00:41:17.995 --> 00:41:21.550
there's links to this at our Probably Management website.

00:41:21.550 --> 00:41:24.410
I think we have links other places around.

00:41:24.410 --> 00:41:25.720
We'll get you to those links.

00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:31.345
It really gives you a nice flavor for how probability management works.

00:41:31.345 --> 00:41:35.475
and I wanna give you an example now.

00:41:35.475 --> 00:41:38.055
When we say rolling up risk,

00:41:38.055 --> 00:41:40.090
one of the great,

00:41:40.090 --> 00:41:43.110
great benefits of using SIPs is that we

00:41:43.110 --> 00:41:47.085
can roll up the results of independent simulations.

00:41:47.085 --> 00:41:49.595
Just think of the two SIPs of the dice.

00:41:49.595 --> 00:41:52.770
Like, I could add the first two elements

00:41:52.770 --> 00:41:56.160
together and the second two and the third down to the 10,000th element.

00:41:56.160 --> 00:42:00.955
I will now have a SIP of the sum of the dice and adding up uncertainties is,

00:42:00.955 --> 00:42:03.060
just not that easy to do.

00:42:03.060 --> 00:42:14.135
So, here's an example now,

00:42:14.135 --> 00:42:16.760
This is not a real live model,

00:42:16.760 --> 00:42:18.290
I mean it is it's real and live,

00:42:18.290 --> 00:42:20.270
but it does not have a real situation.

00:42:20.270 --> 00:42:23.885
It's sort of like a balsa airplane of a model.

00:42:23.885 --> 00:42:28.565
we've got 10,000 trials here.

00:42:28.565 --> 00:42:30.620
we have a bunch of,

00:42:30.620 --> 00:42:34.955
gas pipeline assets,

00:42:34.955 --> 00:42:38.720
pipes, valves, fittings.

00:42:38.720 --> 00:42:45.010
For each asset, I have three SIPs representing financial risk,

00:42:45.010 --> 00:42:47.355
safety risk, reliability risk.

00:42:47.355 --> 00:42:49.570
They're stored in the SIP library.

00:42:49.570 --> 00:42:51.785
You saw what that looks like.

00:42:51.785 --> 00:42:54.910
Now, in Excel, is just pure Excel.

00:42:54.910 --> 00:42:59.545
I'll bet a bunch of you have seen these little filter things, right?

00:42:59.545 --> 00:43:00.970
Where you can actually say, "Oh,

00:43:00.970 --> 00:43:03.640
just show we some of the rows.

00:43:03.640 --> 00:43:07.800
by the way, we've rolled up the total financial risk up here,

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:10.355
the total safety risk,

00:43:10.355 --> 00:43:11.850
the total reliability risk.

00:43:11.850 --> 00:43:14.000
But, let me go to the asset tab, and let me say,

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:18.085
"Hey, let's just look at the pipes."

00:43:18.085 --> 00:43:25.945
Click and that was 10,000 trials now of the conditional risk,

00:43:25.945 --> 00:43:28.430
given that we're just looking at the pipes.

00:43:28.430 --> 00:43:31.300
Now, how do you know if you have a risk model?

00:43:31.300 --> 00:43:34.470
You should be able to tell me that chance of something bad happening.

00:43:34.470 --> 00:43:38.710
So, for example,

00:43:38.710 --> 00:43:43.620
what's the chance that the financial risk is over 40 million?

00:43:43.620 --> 00:43:46.145
it's 100 percent, apparently.

00:43:46.145 --> 00:43:52.080
Okay. What if I change that to 60 million?

00:43:52.080 --> 00:43:55.500
Oh, there's only a 30 percent chance,

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:57.310
that the risk is greater than that,

00:43:57.310 --> 00:43:59.105
that the

00:43:59.105 --> 00:44:03.315
financial loss would be greater than 60 million.

00:44:03.315 --> 00:44:05.255
What about 55 million?

00:44:05.255 --> 00:44:06.710
Seventy-eight percent.

00:44:06.710 --> 00:44:08.640
So, if you can't do that,

00:44:08.640 --> 00:44:10.375
you don't have a risk model.

00:44:10.375 --> 00:44:14.480
You've got to be able to tell me the chances of something happening.

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:23.690
Now, another thing discussed in that article and what I'm showing you now,

00:44:24.160 --> 00:44:28.300
I don't think anyone in the world has fully implemented yet.

00:44:28.300 --> 00:44:31.465
I call it a consolidated risk statement,

00:44:31.465 --> 00:44:35.785
because we're still in pretty early innings with probability management making a lot of

00:44:35.785 --> 00:44:38.395
strides and,

00:44:38.395 --> 00:44:41.610
the technology is changing fast.

00:44:41.610 --> 00:44:44.650
But, here's the idea behind a consolidated risk statement.

00:44:44.650 --> 00:44:50.700
That- again, got three kinds of risks, financial, safety,

00:44:50.700 --> 00:44:55.615
reliability, what I've got,

00:44:55.615 --> 00:44:58.635
I'm going to roll it up across a couple divisions.

00:44:58.635 --> 00:45:02.580
This really has not been possible in the past to easily

00:45:02.580 --> 00:45:06.300
add up the results of different simulations running on different platforms.

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:11.815
Excel, Matlab, R.

00:45:11.815 --> 00:45:15.415
you can do sh*t math with 10,000 people with abacuses.

00:45:15.415 --> 00:45:18.190
The first person would do the first number, the second person will do,

00:45:18.190 --> 00:45:21.300
I mean, it's just a very general open concept.

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:23.040
oh, these divisions?

00:45:23.040 --> 00:45:26.760
Yeah, well, that divisions have business units, right?

00:45:26.760 --> 00:45:31.515
And then, what goes into these risks here?

00:45:31.515 --> 00:45:33.960
Well, of course, the probability distribution is going in,

00:45:33.960 --> 00:45:37.415
and so there's the financial risk,

00:45:37.415 --> 00:45:41.900
the safety risk, the reliability risk,

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:50.705
and let's drill down a little further.

00:45:50.705 --> 00:45:53.739
So, in risk management,

00:45:53.739 --> 00:45:57.805
there's an important concept

00:45:57.805 --> 00:46:01.630
known as the risk exceedance curve,

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:07.935
and it's really just the flip side of a

00:46:07.935 --> 00:46:10.435
probability cumulative curve.

00:46:10.435 --> 00:46:12.555
So, let me zoom in on this.

00:46:12.555 --> 00:46:14.350
Here we have financial risk.

00:46:14.350 --> 00:46:17.105
I'm looking at the blue curve.

00:46:17.105 --> 00:46:20.565
And let me run out here to the blue curve,

00:46:20.565 --> 00:46:23.870
and it tells me that there is

00:46:23.870 --> 00:46:31.480
a 30 percent chance of losing $36.4 million dollars or more.

00:46:31.480 --> 00:46:34.600
Right?

00:46:34.600 --> 00:46:36.840
And that's right here at the 30 percent level.

00:46:36.840 --> 00:46:44.205
now there's a red curve on here as well,

00:46:44.205 --> 00:46:47.165
remember, we said risk is in the eye of the beholder.

00:46:47.165 --> 00:46:51.300
There's a red curve on here that represents your risk tolerance.

00:46:51.300 --> 00:46:55.205
And this means,

00:46:55.205 --> 00:46:57.650
if you go down to this end of the curve,

00:46:57.650 --> 00:47:03.000
that you can tolerate more risk than you actually have.

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:06.300
That's good. But when I get down here,

00:47:06.300 --> 00:47:09.135
and your risk tolerance is below the blue curve,

00:47:09.135 --> 00:47:12.710
then that just says you can't live with that, right?

00:47:12.710 --> 00:47:15.570
Let's see what this red curve says right here.

00:47:15.570 --> 00:47:20.110
Click on that it says there's a 10 percent chance,

00:47:20.110 --> 00:47:27.625
we can tolerate a 10 percent chance of losing $38.3 million.

00:47:27.625 --> 00:47:30.240
Yet but if I go up here,

00:47:30.240 --> 00:47:37.530
we see that there's actually like a 20 percent chance of that, right?

00:47:37.530 --> 00:47:40.770
Now here we have some Olympic analytics going on.

00:47:40.770 --> 00:47:46.565
I'm going to invoke insurance now against that financial risk,

00:47:46.565 --> 00:47:48.575
and watch the curves change.

00:47:48.575 --> 00:47:53.310
Oh, the insurance brought the blue curve down below my red curve,

00:47:53.310 --> 00:47:54.820
so I'm feeling comfortable.

00:47:54.820 --> 00:47:58.050
All right? Now, let's just do a quick time check.

