Okay. So

00:48:08.020 --> 00:48:10.845
this next one

00:48:10.845 --> 00:48:17.680
is an example involving the risk of a project R&amp;D portfolio.

00:48:17.680 --> 00:48:28.010
And so

00:48:28.010 --> 00:48:33.500
this is based on a real-world example that all the numbers

00:48:33.500 --> 00:48:38.800
have been changed in anonymized and we don't have to talk about the actual company.

00:48:38.800 --> 00:48:43.425
But I was approached by folks at one point who said,

00:48:43.425 --> 00:48:47.080
the way we model

00:48:47.080 --> 00:48:51.090
and think of a big R&amp;D pharmaceutical with about 100,

00:48:51.090 --> 00:48:56.215
pharmaceutical products, 100 drugs in the pipeline very, very risky.

00:48:56.215 --> 00:48:58.710
And first of all,

00:48:58.710 --> 00:49:04.170
they had trouble- they could model the finances of any individual drug,

00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:10.270
what they couldn't do was roll them up into a portfolio to understand the portfolio risk.

00:49:10.270 --> 00:49:16.605
And furthermore, there were a lot of portfolio effects.

00:49:16.605 --> 00:49:19.200
This is the kind of thing that SIPs can do very well,

00:49:19.200 --> 00:49:21.905
you can use SIPs to roll up risk.

00:49:21.905 --> 00:49:23.950
Well, one of the issues was this,

00:49:23.950 --> 00:49:30.050
that if you look at the return lifetime revenue of a drug,

00:49:30.050 --> 00:49:31.960
that is very uncertain.

00:49:31.960 --> 00:49:36.130
The bulk of them make almost nothing.

00:49:36.130 --> 00:49:38.720
And there are a few whoppers out here

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:43.655
Viagra and Lipitor, just made tons and tons of money.

00:49:43.655 --> 00:49:47.155
The average drug makes about a billion.

00:49:47.155 --> 00:49:49.575
And so, what they were saying is,

00:49:49.575 --> 00:49:54.580
we've been using this one billion to represent the revenue of a drug,

00:49:54.580 --> 00:49:58.810
but gee, maybe we should be simulating that cause there really is a distribution.

00:49:58.810 --> 00:50:00.880
And I think, yeah, maybe you should.

00:50:00.880 --> 00:50:03.460
so let's take a look at what happened.

00:50:03.460 --> 00:50:08.095
They were simulating a whole bunch of different pharmaceuticals,

00:50:08.095 --> 00:50:10.410
different drugs, and

00:50:10.410 --> 00:50:13.790
they were doing them under a bunch of different

00:50:13.790 --> 00:50:18.300
conditions that affected all the drugs at once like the what's the discount rate?

00:50:18.300 --> 00:50:21.050
What's the probability of success in different classes of drugs?

00:50:21.050 --> 00:50:23.340
And so in particular,

00:50:23.340 --> 00:50:26.350
one of the things we were able to look at is well what would happen

00:50:26.350 --> 00:50:29.665
if you use the one billion average versus you use the full distribution.

00:50:29.665 --> 00:50:35.095
Okay? Let's see if the difference is big enough to view the naked eye.

00:50:35.095 --> 00:50:38.100
by the way, imagine now this is all make-believe numbers,

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:41.105
but suppose our NPV target,

00:50:41.105 --> 00:50:45.065
Net Present Value target is five billion,

00:50:45.065 --> 00:50:49.185
well, right now, it's showing we have a 97 percent chance of us achieving that target.

00:50:49.185 --> 00:50:52.565
But that's because we're using a single average over here.

00:50:52.565 --> 00:50:55.660
Okay? Now, I'm gonna use the full distribution, and by the way,

00:50:55.660 --> 00:51:00.035
this is gonna be 5,000 trials when I click this over 100,

00:51:00.035 --> 00:51:02.180
drugs many, many experiments.

00:51:02.180 --> 00:51:05.110
It's just like 300 million numbers running through this model.

00:51:05.110 --> 00:51:08.935
Okay. I'm gonna click the full distribution and see if you can tell the difference, okay?

00:51:08.935 --> 00:51:15.475
Full distribution of drug prices one billion average instead of the full distribution.

00:51:15.475 --> 00:51:18.675
So, the average for- okay, can you tell the difference?

00:51:18.675 --> 00:51:20.705
Okay, now shut your eyes, your left eye.

00:51:20.705 --> 00:51:24.170
Okay. Is that better or is that better?

00:51:24.170 --> 00:51:26.300
Oh, and by the way, check over here,

00:51:26.300 --> 00:51:33.505
the chance of achieving your five billion target went from 97 percent to 44 percent.

00:51:33.505 --> 00:51:37.970
Okay? Ninety-seven percent to 44 percent.

