00:52:03.350 --> 00:52:06.070
So, the first question is,

00:52:06.070 --> 00:52:08.840
your presentation was incredibly engaging,

00:52:08.840 --> 00:52:10.770
can you talk about what the course will be like?

00:52:10.770 --> 00:52:12.085
How will it be structured,

00:52:12.085 --> 00:52:15.050
and what kinds of assignments will there be?

00:52:15.050 --> 00:52:21.165
So,

00:52:21.165 --> 00:52:22.970
none of my successes have been planned,

00:52:22.970 --> 00:52:24.540
and none of my plans have been successful.

00:52:24.540 --> 00:52:27.190
that's why I do stochastic modeling, right?

00:52:27.190 --> 00:52:28.990
Life is uncertain.

00:52:28.990 --> 00:52:31.025
The class will depend

00:52:31.025 --> 00:52:32.650
on the students who take it.

00:52:32.650 --> 00:52:35.340
But it will be project-based.

00:52:35.340 --> 00:52:37.480
So, one of the first things we're gonna

00:52:37.480 --> 00:52:41.200
learn is what I call the arithmetic of uncertainty.

00:52:41.200 --> 00:52:44.745
And it will all be done in Excel,

00:52:44.745 --> 00:52:47.350
and virtually every week,

00:52:47.350 --> 00:52:48.630
you'll have a new Excel,

00:52:48.630 --> 00:52:50.945
projects to do.

00:52:50.945 --> 00:52:56.035
But what I really want to do is have students go out and search for real-world projects.

00:52:56.035 --> 00:52:59.675
I am in negotiations now with some,

00:52:59.675 --> 00:53:02.530
with some companies that have project data,

00:53:02.530 --> 00:53:04.495
and I'd like to

00:53:04.495 --> 00:53:08.015
to have the students try to create SIP libraries.

00:53:08.015 --> 00:53:10.675
So that, for example,

00:53:10.675 --> 00:53:13.635
